# The normalisation map: the fields on which the GTK target and the Rune target may differ
# without that being a divergence. One rule to a line, sorted by field:
#
#   <field> <exception code> <issue>
#
# A field is named as an observation names it, with [] for every element of a list:
# app.focused, app.panes[].selected, fs, settings.show_hidden. A rule covers the field and
# everything below it. The codes are the plan's exceptions: timing, a11y, rune, oracle,
# toolkit, deviation, platform. A pull request adds only its own rules, and every fuzz
# summary prints how many there are. Noise that two replays on one target show is not an
# exception: it goes in calibration.mask. See docs/testing.md, "Fuzzing".
#
# A rule may name the values it excuses after its issue, {"gtk":<value>,"rune":[<value>, …]}:
# it then leaves out only GTK's value against one of Rune's on its field.
# Without "rune" it leaves out GTK's value against any of Rune's, and without "gtk" any of GTK's
# against one of Rune's. With "while":{"gtk":{<field>:<value>, …},"rune":{…}} it applies only at
# a step at which each side's observation holds those values: a field is a key of app, or of
# the context, which nothing compares (context.toplevels, the windows open beside the browser;
# context.window_focused, whether anything in the window has the keyboard), and a value one
# value or {"one_of":[<value>, …]}. A rule may then name neither side's values, and leaves
# out every difference on its field there. Rules of one field, code and issue are sorted by what
# follows the issue.
# On a list, {"rune_adds":[<value>, …]} leaves out only a Rune list that is GTK's with those
# elements added. The rune_adds rules on one field are taken out together, so a list that adds
# what two rulings allow, as an audio player's "Volume" and "Album art", is GTK's with both
# taken out.
# A plain popover is a named dialog on Rune and an unnamed panel on GTK, by the owner's ruling
# of 2026-11-13 (owner.md): only its name against GTK's none is excused, never a real dialog.
# GTK reports `null` for its popover whether it is open or not, so this rule cannot catch a
# Rune popover left open where GTK's closed; the fields after it, focus and the panes, do.
app.dialog deviation lgse/strata#2405 {"gtk":null,"rune":["Appearance","Jobs","Thumbnail size"]}
# Settings is a named dialog on Rune and an unnamed layer on GTK, and its choice menus are menus
# on Rune and unnamed popovers on GTK, by the owner's ruling of 2026-11-17 (owner.md): only
# "Settings" against GTK's none, and an open choice menu's [] against GTK's none, are excused.
# A message over Settings ("Reset all keybinds?", the save notice) needs no rule: Rune's
# snapshot names the dialog on top, as GTK's does (`nests_dialogs`, the same ruling).
# app.menu is the menu items of the first open menu, so [] is a menu of radio or check rows
# only. Of Rune's menus today only Settings' choice menus are one where GTK has none: Sort by is
# a menu on both, and the List columns menu has a menu item. A new Rune menu of radio or check
# rows over a GTK popover needs its own ruling. As for #2405's rule, GTK reports `null` whether
# Settings or its menu is open or not, so these rules cannot catch a Rune one left open where
# GTK's closed; the focus fields after them do.
app.dialog deviation lgse/strata#2572 {"gtk":null,"rune":["Settings"]}
# After the floating Copy to or Move to chooser closes, GTK's browser window is not active
# again on the QA's Xvfb, which runs no window manager to do it, so GTK reports no focus at all;
# on a desktop both interfaces leave the keyboard in the browser, and Rune's QA host gives it
# back as a window manager does. The owner ruled on 2026-11-17 (owner.md) that this is excused
# here, `toolkit`: GTK's focus follows GDK's X11 window activation. The first four rules apply
# only while GTK's whole window has no keyboard (context.window_focused false: no mapped widget
# in it has focus, as in an inactive window, or just after the focused widget was unmapped) and
# its browser no focused pane, and excuse only GTK's no focus against Rune's:
# - app.focused and app.panes[].focused: GTK's null against any row of Rune's;
# - app.focused_pane: GTK's null against any pane of Rune's;
# - app.directory, which is the focused pane's, or the last pane's when none is focused: any
#   value of Rune's against GTK's.
# GTK's keyboard on the sidebar, the header, the location bar or a dialog leaves its window
# focused, so these rules do not apply there and Rune's focus on a row still counts. The map
# names no step, so they hold for as long as GTK's window has no keyboard: from the chooser's close
# until a later chooser on the bare Xvfb. What they could hide is a Rune focus bug at such a
# step: the keyboard on the wrong row, or on a row where it should be elsewhere. No scenario
# reads the browser's focus after the floating chooser closes:
# `floating-chooser-transfers-files/*` and `compress-then-chooser-extracts/columns` check the
# files only, and `abandoned-extract-to-no-hijack/columns-cancelled-password` shows only that
# a key reaches the browser's password prompt then (line 24). Rune's own coverage is
# `qa::tests::floating::the_floating_chooser_is_a_toplevel_in_front_of_the_browser` (the
# browser has the keys again after Escape) and `window::qa`'s raise and close.
# The second four, by the owner's ruling of 2026-11-18, excuse the other way round: from the
# second chooser of a sequence on, GTK's browser is active again on the bare Xvfb and reports
# its row while the chooser is open, where Rune's has no focus behind its chooser, as an
# inactive window has none. They apply only while Rune's browser has no focused pane and
# Rune shows a floating chooser open (context.toplevels is ["Copy to"], ["Move to"] or
# ["Extract to"]), and excuse only Rune's null against any value of GTK's:
# - app.focused and app.panes[].focused: any row of GTK's against Rune's null;
# - app.focused_pane: any pane of GTK's against Rune's null;
# - app.directory: any value of GTK's against Rune's.
# A Rune browser that kept the keyboard behind its chooser still counts: its focus is then no
# null. So does a Rune browser that lost the keyboard with no chooser open. What they could
# hide is GTK's browser keeping the keyboard behind a chooser on a desktop, which no target here
# can show, and a wrong pane or row of GTK's while Rune's chooser is open. Rune's side is held
# by `qa::tests::floating::the_floating_chooser_is_a_toplevel_in_front_of_the_browser` (the
# browser has no focus while the chooser is in front, and the keys again after Escape) and
# `window::qa`'s raise and close.
app.directory toolkit lgse/strata#2595 {"while":{"gtk":{"app.focused_pane":null,"context.window_focused":false}}}
app.directory toolkit lgse/strata#2595 {"while":{"rune":{"app.focused_pane":null,"context.toplevels":{"one_of":[["Copy to"],["Move to"],["Extract to"]]}}}}
app.focused toolkit lgse/strata#2595 {"gtk":null,"while":{"gtk":{"app.focused_pane":null,"context.window_focused":false}}}
app.focused toolkit lgse/strata#2595 {"rune":[null],"while":{"rune":{"app.focused_pane":null,"context.toplevels":{"one_of":[["Copy to"],["Move to"],["Extract to"]]}}}}
app.focused_pane toolkit lgse/strata#2595 {"gtk":null,"while":{"gtk":{"context.window_focused":false}}}
app.focused_pane toolkit lgse/strata#2595 {"rune":[null],"while":{"rune":{"context.toplevels":{"one_of":[["Copy to"],["Move to"],["Extract to"]]}}}}
# app.menu's rule is lgse/strata#2572's, with app.dialog's above.
app.menu deviation lgse/strata#2572 {"gtk":null,"rune":[[]]}
# app.panes[].focused's rule is lgse/strata#2595's, with app.focused's above.
app.panes[].focused toolkit lgse/strata#2595 {"gtk":null,"while":{"gtk":{"app.focused_pane":null,"context.window_focused":false}}}
app.panes[].focused toolkit lgse/strata#2595 {"rune":[null],"while":{"rune":{"app.focused_pane":null,"context.toplevels":{"one_of":[["Copy to"],["Move to"],["Extract to"]]}}}}
# A PDF's page is one image on GTK; Rune's PDF view also names the document, each page and the
# page's text layer, by the owner's ruling of 2026-11-19 (owner.md): only those three of the
# fixture's PDF, tests/fixtures/documents/text.pdf, a page of one, are excused, wherever
# they stand, and every other name of the drawer, in its order, still counts.
app.preview deviation lgse/strata#2560 {"rune_adds":["PDF document","Page 1 of 1","Hello PDF text layer\nsecond line here"]}
# The preview media player's volume slider is named "Volume" on Rune and unnamed on GTK, by the
# owner's ruling of 2026-11-18 (owner.md): Rune's app.preview is GTK's with "Volume" added, and
# only that is excused. Every other name of the drawer, and their order, still counts.
app.preview deviation lgse/strata#2606 {"rune_adds":["Volume"]}
# The audio player's art and spectrum are named "Album art" and "Audio spectrum" on both
# targets, but GTK sets their names with update_property rather than accessibility::set_label,
# so GTK's QA snapshot, which reads only the names the helper records, reads them unnamed
# (lgse/strata#2612, an oracle finding): only those two names are excused.
app.preview oracle lgse/strata#2612 {"rune_adds":["Album art","Audio spectrum"]}
